{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on ownership-descent proposal 4ea5e41c126e00c131e6edd16cd0b31e33df1c5f. The owner-versus-kind split is right, and decisions one and two close direct ratifier multiplication if the current role checks remain. Six design gaps need pinning before adoption.\n\n1. Ownership is not the existing membership-basis rule applied one level up. In internal/workroom/fold.go:668-724, membershipBasis is populated only for a non-membership authority grant, is checked against the grantee's own actor key, and is ignored whenever grant.membership is true. membershipGrantActive at 737-743 checks only direct activity, not recursive membership activity. Ordinary roster membership is added only when ratified (350-372); only the genesis seed is added directly. Unilateral agent minting therefore needs an explicit new roster transition plus an owner actor/event field and recursive, cycle-safe membership activity. Call that extension out rather than claiming no new primitive.\n\n2. Retired-minter and in-flight semantics are undefined and do not come free. decideState at fold.go:412-451 does not generally require the author to be a live participant. The application pre-append gate is the static local Config.Actors allowlist (app.go:824-831), and RetireActor removes only the named actor's custody entry (519-575), not descendants. A descendant whose membership is projected inactive can therefore still have an allowlisted key and append effective ordinary statements or requests to other live actors; a request signed before retirement but appended after it is indistinguishable because the sequence has no trusted signing-time order. Pin the rule (the defensible one is sequence order: descendant acts appended after the ownership basis retires are ineffective), how descendant custody is handled, and whether already-effective descendant statements merely remain historical or become stale. The latter requires an implicit membership dependency for acts; they do not currently cite one.\n\n3. 'Starts with no roles' must say 'no authority roles.' Membership is the participant role (fold.go:691-701, 746-756), and request validity depends on it. The current ratifier check at 644-649 and the self-promotion regression at fold_test.go:917-939 do block a roleless participant from ratifying its own operator grant. Agents can still mint effective assertions/requests if live, so ownership should be displayed anywhere counts could otherwise look like independent consensus.\n\n4. Rotation is not yet an actor-level analogue of sequencer rotation. Actor identity is the key fingerprint and local Actor stores only Name, Fingerprint, KeyFile (app.go:28-32); there is no actor-key rotation event preserving a principal across fingerprints. Naming the minting membership event avoids naming a key, but it does not say which rotated fingerprint may exercise the old owner's authority. Either define actor continuity/rotation here or remove decision four from this proposal and make it a prerequisite child design.\n\n5. Caller-relative bare-name resolution is circular for signer selection. Actor and ResolveActorAddress currently resolve a local name/fingerprint with no caller context (app.go:437-443, 749-761). The caller cannot be used to resolve `--as` before the caller itself is known. Separate exact signer selection from caller-relative target resolution, and define whether agents may mint descendants, whether qualification uses the direct chain or human root, how duplicate qualified labels are refused, and what retired owners/children do to lookup. ActorName currently emits only the bare projected name (statusview/view.go:118-127), so display qualification is a separate required surface.\n\n6. A ProfileVersion bump is cache invalidation, not a durable cutover. schema.go:13-20 binds checkpoints, but without an activation event/prefix boundary a new ownerless roster statement is indistinguishable from a grandfathered old root. Pin a ratified activation event or other durable sequence position: memberships before it may derive self-ownership; memberships after it must carry and validate owner evidence. New readers then reject old-profile checkpoints and full-audit under the new rule; old readers fail closed on the new wire shape. Tests must cover an attached auditor with an old checkpoint, a post-activation ownerless grant, and the three existing roots. Consider an explicit ratified re-parent path for existing agents; permanent self-rooting preserves compatibility but gives the current workroom none of the proposed accountability benefit.\n\nWith those changes the proposal would have a reviewable security model. As written, retirement and migration in particular can produce actors that look dead in the projection while their later acts still take effect.","body":{"proposal":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4ea5e41c126e00c131e6edd16cd0b31e33df1c5f","tests":"design review only; code inspected on current main 8d08315a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}